1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
 It's 9/36 and time for staff breakfast from B&B Pharmacy with Warren Hasse.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 Well many people say that flying is not as dangerous as driving an automobile.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 Others say you never get me up one of those things.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 And then others say well I'd like to go to the moon and others say never.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 So it doesn't matter how you look at it, everybody's got a different opinion about aviation.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
 Personally we think it's the greatest thing since sliced bread really.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 But this morning a gentleman who is interested in aviation

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 and makes a living worrying about the possibilities of aircraft accidents

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 and a very responsible role in that regard is our guest on staff breakfast.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 He's here visiting friends and relatives, Mr. Bobby Ray Allen.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
 Bobby Ray, good to have you with us this morning.

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 Thank you very much, good to be here.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 And let me first, let's run through that long title that you have once again, which is what?

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
 The title is Special Assistant to the Director of the Bureau of Aviation Safety

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 of the National Transportation Safety Board.

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
 Well other than that, we just about have time for the closing conversion.

17
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 Not that we got that in.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 Which sounds like it does have a great deal to do with aviation safety, is that right?

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 Yes, the board is the agency established by Congress

20
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 to investigate all aircraft accidents throughout the United States

21
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
 and attempt to determine probable cause and prevent the recurrence of aircraft accidents.

22
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 And you're not here in any official capacity right now?

23
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
 No, we're visiting relatives here.

24
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 I have two very favorite uncles living in the area, Mr. Marion Brown and Mr. Edgar Brown.

25
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 Well I just checked him out and he knows what he's talking about.

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 I showed him an aircraft accident which I was in a few years ago

27
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 and he took a look at the picture and said it looks like this is what happened

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 and you're absolutely right, that's what did happen.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 He had it figured out very well.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 What is this percentage of aircraft safety versus automobile safety?

31
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 Could you give us any idea on that? What's your thinking?

32
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 Well it's very difficult to make a valid comparison.

33
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 In many respects there is no valid comparison.

34
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 But on the highways each year there are slightly over 50,000 people killed

35
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 and in aviation in the United States there are approximately 1200 killed each year.

36
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 In commercial aviation there will be something on the order of 250 to 300 average

37
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 and in private aviation there will be approximately 1000 fatalities a year.

38
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 And of course the auto industry or the various departments of public safety

39
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
 I think base their ratio on a per mile driven ratio.

40
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 Do you figure yours on an airline mileage ratio of any chance?

41
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 There's been a lot of controversy on the yardstick for establishing it right over the past several years.

42
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
 One group would use aircraft hours flown.

43
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
 Another group would use the yardstick of passenger miles flown.

44
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 And I suppose that I prefer to use the aircraft hours flown

45
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 or in better yet one mission, one take off the landing

46
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 or take off your en route cruise to your destination and landing.

47
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 Because it'd be from here to Amral or here to Los Angeles regardless of distance.

48
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 Why do you say that? What's your thinking on that?

49
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 Well I think the greatest amount of exposure occurs during take off or landing.

50
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 And if you're going to be actually any time you attempt to establish an accident rate

51
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 or a fatality rate in any mode of transportation you have to take into consideration the exposure.

52
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 And the greatest exposure occurs during take off or during landing.

53
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 And that's the reason that I would like to see a rate based on each mission or each flight

54
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 rather than just passenger miles flown.

55
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 Then you're saying that there's not a great deal of possibility of midair crashes.

56
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 Of course it exists but the possibility is fairly remote is it not?

57
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 Well, I started speech one time and said that we had the potential or the problem of midair collisions

58
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 ever since Will returned to Orville and said let's build another one.

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 I think that right now the midair collision problem is being discussed throughout the entire United States.

60
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 I think that it can't help but, the problem can't help but increase because our civil aviation fleet

61
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 is projected to increase to approximately 260,000 airplanes in the next 10 years.

62
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
 Now that's doubling the fleet that we have today.

63
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 Right now we have approximately 120,000 to 130,000 airplanes in the civil aviation fleet.

64
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 And out of this 130,000 aircraft approximately 27 to 2800 are involved in commercial aviation.

65
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 Now by the year 1980 they expect the civil aviation fleet to increase to approximately 260,000 airplanes.

66
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 But the commercial transport fleet will only increase to about 3100 airplanes.

67
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:35,000
 But the size of those, the loads that they can carry will be much greater, will they not?

68
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
 Yes, the Q7-47 that's, well, let's just enjoy it.

69
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 It's made in flight here about two months ago and in Seattle, Washington is capable of carrying about 500 passengers.

70
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
 Unbelievable. Unbelievable.

71
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:00,000
 I couldn't believe it last December our band and choir here at PAM high school had the privilege of performing.

72
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
 Well actually I'm national television on both coasts at football games.

73
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:09,000
 But they flew to New York in an airliner that seated at that time to carry 250 passengers.

74
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 I didn't think there was anything that big, but there was.

75
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
 It's huge. I can't even start to visualize when I'll carry 500.

76
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 The Lockheed aircraft is building a new jetliner called the Kim 11, I believe it is.

77
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
 And the passenger carrying capacity of that aircraft will be about 350.

78
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
 This is one they refer to as the Airbus.

79
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:41,000
 An interesting comparison on the 747, if you land 3 747s with a full passenger load at an airport,

80
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 it would be almost the same as disembarking the passengers on the Queen Mary.

81
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 So this will give you some feel for the impact the 747 is going to have on your airports around the country.

82
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 That's tremendous. I imagine there's an area that a lot of people are not familiar with and don't understand how it's accomplished.

83
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:11,000
 We might discuss this business of routes and altitudes and so on and so forth.

84
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
 Your automobiles today on our super highways, Interstate 40, for instance, is divided.

85
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:20,000
 One and two lanes go in one direction and there's a divider between and two lanes going the other direction.

86
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:28,000
 You have somewhat similar or comparable flying rules and regulations, do you not, for east and west traffic and so on?

87
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:34,000
 Yes, there again. Actually, I suppose you'd say there are two sets of rules.

88
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
 You have a set of rules that prevail when the weather is good, no instrument conditions.

89
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,000
 And then you have the rules that exist under instrument weather conditions.

90
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:55,000
 And under your IFR or instrument rules, altitude and traffic control is accomplished with their traffic control system.

91
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:08,000
 And under the visual flight rules or IFR conditions, the traffic flow is pretty much left in the cockpit of the aircraft subject to the rules that exist.

92
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:17,000
 These rules are that if you're flying north or south, you will fly at an odd or even a thousand foot plus or minus 500 feet.

93
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:30,000
 And east and west, I believe the rule is called the quadrennial rule. And then the responsibility for maintaining a lookout for other aircraft rests in the cockpit.

94
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 There's a tremendous amount of assistance that would be available to the pilots from the FAA air traffic control system.

95
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
 But the pilot has to avail himself from this.

96
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:54,000
 In your job, do you personally go out on the scene of aircraft accidents for investigation or do you send teams out or is that at all in the area of your position?

97
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 Yes, I have not personally investigated an accident for several years. I started out with the board as a field investigator working out of the Chicago office.

98
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:23,000
 But on a air carrier accident or a catastrophic accident involving passengers, we usually dispatch a team from Washington D.C. that's comprised of specialists in various areas.

99
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:32,000
 Power plants, systems, human factors, operations, engineering, flight recorder, readout.

100
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:39,000
 And each individual is responsible for a particular phase of the investigation.

101
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:49,000
 Now on a general aviation accident investigation, this is generally investigated by two people, two men.

102
00:09:49,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 As I mentioned earlier, the large team type investigation in the team, in some instances, is comprised of 40 to 50 people. And this is primarily the difference.

103
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:12,000
 Speaking of something like the collision over Grand Canyon, that's where your big teams go out and work on it.

104
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:23,000
 Historically, over the past 10 years, we've had on the order of about 12 what we call team type investigations a year.

105
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:37,000
 It'll run anywhere from 8 to 14 major catastrophic accidents a year. And that number has remained fairly static since about 1955 or '56.

106
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:48,000
 Well really, any time you have an airplane accident, it's an amount to be in catastrophic, I guess, at least certainly in commercial aviation.

107
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:52,000
 There's no question because there's always a large number of board and it's a big airplane.

108
00:10:52,000 --> 00:11:01,000
 You take a little small airplane, monier, piper, patchy, something like that, where you've only got one or two passengers.

109
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 It's just like an automobile accident where it takes one or two lives.

110
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:12,000
 You have to be very careful when you talk about a catastrophic accident. It's a matter of personal concern and involvement.

111
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:19,000
 I recall I was in an accident one time and as far as I was concerned, I was the only one aboard the aircraft and it was catastrophic.

112
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,000
 Surely, absolutely. I'm saying it's true. It was an automobile accident, I believe that's certainly true.

113
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:37,000
 You've also had another big responsibility. You were on an American United States team which was working with the Russians concerning the airline, commercial airline, between New York and Moscow.

114
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,000
 And I think that'd be interesting to find out how that worked out, what some of the problems were that you had to overcome.

115
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
 And I'd like to discuss that with you right after we pause for these words.

116
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:51,000
 Our guest is Bobby Ray Allen, gentlemen who's here visiting relatives in half-accidents for the government.

117
00:11:51,000 --> 00:12:00,000
 And I'm interested, Bobby, let's talk about this situation between Russia and the United States and the commercial lines that were open between Moscow and New York.

118
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
 There were lots of problems to be worked out there where they're not.

119
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 Yes, the original negotiation that the Russians established commercial esters between New York and Moscow started back in 1960. And you may recall that in 1961 there was a rogue international affair called the Berlin Crisis.

120
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:31,000
 And our negotiation with that time broke down. As a matter of fact, my personal life was interrupted at that time. I was recalled in Navy.

121
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:53,000
 And then the negotiations were resumed in 1966. The basic agreement had been signed back in 1960. But the details of how the air service would be implemented had not been worked out.

122
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:03,000
 And so in 1966 President Johnson started the negotiations off again.

123
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:15,000
 And the recent negotiations consisted of two meetings. One that would be held in Moscow and the U.S. contingent would travel to Moscow.

124
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:25,000
 And then the second phase of the negotiations would be the meeting in New York City where the Russian delegation would travel to the United States.

125
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:43,000
 Our group consisted of about 20 people. And it covered just one phase of the negotiations with facilities to navigational facilities to certification.

126
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:53,000
 In my particular area of responsibility was to establish the ground rules for the investigation of an accident and the unlikely event of one should occur.

127
00:13:53,000 --> 00:14:05,000
 And we traveled to Moscow in January of 1967 and remained there for a period of about six weeks.

128
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:27,000
 And during that period we were given permission to invest here to expand and look at the facilities at Timoshibo Airport here in Moscow. And then we looked at the ultimate airport located up at Victory.

129
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:37,000
 And I was somewhat surprised when I expected that the Russians would not permit us to go into the radar room.

130
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:45,000
 And a lot of the facilities there they might be somewhat reluctant to let us look.

131
00:14:45,000 --> 00:15:01,000
 But much to my surprise when we arrived here they let us have a very cooperative approach. We expected the what would be equivalent of our air traffic control center.

132
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 Looked at their radar installations. And once they had told us that we could go into the radar room I had expected that they would turn the radar to stand by or turn it off.

133
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:27,000
 And as we walked in the suite was operating. Very impressive. I was very much impressed with the technical know-how and ability of the Russian people.

134
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:32,000
 Are we comparable in that respect? Or could you make any kind of estimate?

135
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:50,000
 I don't really think that I could make a comparison primarily because I think you have to realize that what I saw was probably the best that they had to offer as far as commercial air service was concerned.

136
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:01,000
 We did not travel into some of their factories or some of the other facilities that would give you an opportunity to make a valid comparison.

137
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:18,000
 I guess maybe based on personal projection that I'm convinced that they surpassed us. But certainly they don't want to give up some of their accomplishments.

138
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:28,000
 As for instance some of the things that you worked out I assume let's suppose that a Russian airliner crashes that's bound for the United States crashes in Russia.

139
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:39,000
 Will a Russian team investigate? Those are the kind of things that you had to work out and if an American plane were to crash in Russia who would investigate?

140
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:43,000
 Would it be a joint team? Are those the problems that came up?

141
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:59,000
 Yes, let me see if I can clarify this a little more. There's an organization in Montreal called the International Civil Aviation Organization. Now there are approximately 109 countries throughout the world that belong to that particular organization.

142
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:15,000
 Now by membership in that organization and by agreeing to abide by the rules that have been established by the International Civil Aviation Organization it's not necessary to negotiate a bilateral agreement with the member countries.

143
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:32,000
 Unfortunately Russia back in 1944 decided not to join the International Civil Aviation Organization. Consequently it became necessary for us to negotiate each facet of this air service on an individual basis.

144
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:48,000
 And what we did was we arrived in an agreement with the Russians that we would abide by most of the provisions of annex 13 to the Chicago Convention or the IKO manuals.

145
00:17:48,000 --> 00:18:09,000
 And in the case of an American aircraft crashing in Russia, the Russian would investigate the accident as the state of occurrence and we would be permitted to participate with the Russians in the investigation as the state of registry and as the state of manufacture.

146
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:15,000
 And if a Russian aircraft crashed in the United States it would work exactly the same way.

147
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:28,000
 So those are the things that have to be worked out. It's good that you've gotten the work done. I think there's no problems now hopefully and we hope nothing ever happens.

148
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
 I hope we never have to use this.

149
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:45,000
 Bobby you I would assume would be an avid reader of aviation or aircraft books stories about flying the latest big hit airport had to be of interest to you I'm sure.

150
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:46,000
 Yes.

151
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:54,000
 What was that one where this airliner and this military jet were on a crash course, collision course. Can't think of the name of that one. Do you remember that book?

152
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:04,000
 Oh that was a book that was written by Ernest Gayan and you caught me short here. I can't think of the title of it either but it was an exciting one, an exciting movie.

153
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
 A Long High and the Mighty. Was that it? No.

154
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
 No that was B-17 story.

155
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:09,000
 That's right.

156
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
 You're correct. It was an Ernest Gayan book.

157
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:30,000
 But it's an area in which I assume with the success that we've had of several of these that we will have many more fine novels written. Actually, since you mentioned Orville and Wilbur, I believe we're just barely into the crawling stage of aviation.

158
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:56,000
 It's rather interesting when you consider that the first flight at Kitty Hawk occurred on December the 17th in 1903. That's only 66 years ago and we've gone from their first flight in the old bywing airplane that they built there to 747 and fuselage of the 747 is longer than the total distance of their first flight.

159
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:07,000
 Amazing. Amazing. Bobby, all you people are in the aviation industry and you've been in it, you've studied it and know it forward and backward pretty much I would assume.

160
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:18,000
 And you get around in bold sessions. You've been able to sit here through moon missions, these various launches that we've had. I imagine a lot of people say, "Man, I wish I was on that."

161
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:27,000
 And the next one says, "Not me." Or do they? Would everybody that's involved in your work like to be on one of those jaunts to the moon?

162
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:34,000
 A few years ago, I probably would have given my teeth ago. I'd think about it a little while now, I guess.

163
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:48,000
 And now it's proven that it can be done and before it wasn't. You'd rather have gone before you were convinced. It still sounds exciting though. And aviation, that field certainly is and it's growing.

164
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:56,000
 The statistics that you passed on to us earlier about the growth and private aviation and commercial aviation are fantastic.

165
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:06,000
 The communities such as ours right here in Tampa realizing the necessity of better airfields, better aviation facilities.

166
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:17,000
 And we're over time here, Bobby. I want to go back to one thing that passed through my mind. We were talking about these air corridors and you mentioned the instrument flights.

167
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:26,000
 In many, many, many times you come down into a city, especially up in the east, I guess, where you've got more bad weather than we do here, where you land by instrument.

168
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:41,000
 And then you come down through clouds from 10,000 and then break out at 700 feet or something like that. To those who have never done it or have only done it once or twice, it's quite a thrill. How safe are we under those conditions?

169
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:53,000
 I think the instrument landing system that we have, if you have a full instrument landing facility at an airport, there's no major in it at all.

170
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:59,000
 The rut is highly perfected and the equipment is very reliable.

171
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:08,000
 It certainly is. It's amazing. These boys will come out and there they are right on, you can see the end of the runway down there a quarter of a mile or a half mile away and they're in perfect alignment.

172
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:17,000
 It's truly amazing and more work is being done constantly to further assure the safety of passengers as it must.

173
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:27,000
 Bobby, our thanks to you so much for coming down and being with us on staff breakfast this morning. It's been very interesting bringing out these points about flying. Let me ask us, where's home for you?

174
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:33,000
 Well, home presently is in Fort Worth. I was originally born in Winsborough, Texas and grew up in Marshall, Texas.

175
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:43,000
 And I was going to ask, being that close though, I guess you drove out here and you didn't fly. Good. Thank you very much, Bobby Ray Allen for being with us on staff breakfast this morning.

